Improve the code written in Basics of Multi Threading Part 1
exercise question 4 to handle the deadlock using reentract lock.

Approach:
    Two Resources:
        Pen
        Paper

    Two Threads:
        t1 (User1): Wants to use Pen first, then Paper.
        t2 (User2): Wants to use Paper first, then Pen.

    Potential Deadlock Issue:
        If t1 locks the Pen and t2 locks the Paper, both will wait for each other, causing a deadlock.
        To prevent deadlock, the program uses tryLock() instead of a regular lock.

Breakdown of Execution

    User1 (t1) tries to get the Pen:
        If successful, it then tries to get the Paper.
        If it can't get Paper, it releases the Pen and exits.

    User2 (t2) tries to get the Paper:
        If successful, it then tries to get the Pen.
        If it can't get Pen, it releases the Paper and exits.

    If a thread fails to acquire both locks, it just prints a message and avoids waiting indefinitely.



ReentrantLock: Allows fine-grained control over locking/unlocking.
tryLock(): Prevents deadlock by trying to acquire a lock instead of waiting indefinitely.
Thread Coordination (join()) ensures main() waits for both threads to finish.
